<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    
  
  
  
  </problem></exercise>
</example><!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
<exercise class="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
    
    <para>Explain how your work in the previous questions can help you mentally evaluate \(22 \cdot 18\).</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your explanation.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(22 \cdot 18\) can be written as \((20 + 2)(20 - 2)\), which is equivalent to \(202 - 22\), which is \(400 - 4\), or 396.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction-->

<exercise class="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <para>Explain how your work in the previous questions can help you mentally evaluate \(45 \cdot 35\).</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your explanation.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(45 \cdot 35\) can be written as \((40 + 5)(40 - 5)\), which is equivalent to \(40^2-5^2\), which is \(1600 - 25\), or 1575.</para>
    
    
  
  </solution></exercise>

<exercise class="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <para>Here is a shortcut that can be used to mentally square any two-digit number. Let's take \(83^2\), for example.</para>
    
    <para>83 is \(80 + 3\).</para>
      <para>Compute \(80^2\) and \(3^2\), which gives 6400 and 9. Add these values to get 6409.</para>
      <para>Compute \(80 \cdot 3\), which is 240. Double it to get 480.</para>
      <para>Add 6409 and 480 to get 6889.</para>
    <para>Try using this method to find the squares of some other two-digit numbers. (With some practice, it is possible to get really fast at this!)</para>
    
    <para>Explain why this method works.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your explanation.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Any two-digit number can be written as \(a + b\), where \(a\) is 10 times the first digit and \(b\) is the second digit. We are interested in squaring \(a + b\):</para>
    
    <para>\((a + b)^2=(a + b)(a + b)\)</para>
    
    <para>\(= a^2+ab +ab +b^2\) by the distributive property</para>
    
    <para>\(= a^2+ 2ab +b^2\) by combining like terms</para>
    
    <para>\(= a^2+b^2+2ab\) because addition is commutative</para>
    
    
    
    <para>We get the \(a^2 + b^2\) from squaring \(a\) and \(b\) and adding them, and the \(2ab\) from multiplying the two values and doubling them.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction--></section>


<note class="self-check">
  <para>Which of the following is equivalent to \(9^2 − 6^2\)?</para>
</note>

<section><title>Recognizing the Expanded Product of the Difference of Two Squares</title>
  <para>Mathematically, something special happens when two binomials that are conjugates are multiplied. In other words, the product of an expression of the form (\(a - b)(a + b)\) results in a unique expression called a difference of two squares. Here are some examples of these special products.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
  <para>Let's look at the expression \((12 + 5)(12 − 5)\). We want to determine if it is equivalent to \(12^2−5^2\).</para>
  <para>We can use the distributive property of multiplication over addition, or FOIL process, to multiply.</para>
  <para>\((12 + 5)(12 − 5)\) 
    
    \(\begin{array}{rcl} &amp;=&amp;12^2-12\left(5\right)+5\left(12\right)-5^2\\&amp;&amp;\\&amp;=&amp;12^2-60+60-5^2\\&amp;&amp;\\&amp;=&amp;12^2-5^2\\&amp;&amp;\end{array}\)
    So, \((12 + 5)(12 − 5)\) is equivalent to \(12^2-5^2\).
  </para>
  <para><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
  <para>Let's look at a different example using a <term class="glossary-tooltip">variable</term>.</para>
  <para>\((x + 7)(x − 7)\)</para>
  <para>Is this equivalent to \(x^2−7^2\)?</para>
  <para>\((x + 7)(x − 7)\)
    
    \(\begin{array}{rcl} &amp;=&amp;x^2-7x+7x-7^2\\&amp;&amp;\\&amp;=&amp;x^2-7^2=x^2-49\\&amp;&amp;\end{array}\)
    This can also be shown using a diagram.
  </para>
  <table class="os-raise-doubleheadertable">
    
    <tgroup cols="3"><tbody><row>
        <entry><emphasis/></entry><entry><emphasis>
         \(x\)
       </emphasis> </entry><entry><emphasis>
          –7
       </emphasis> </entry></row><row>
        <entry><emphasis>
          \(x\)
        
       </emphasis> </entry><entry>
          \(x^2\)
        </entry>
        <entry>
          \(-7x\)
        </entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry><emphasis>
          7
       </emphasis> </entry><entry>
         \(7x\)
        </entry>
        <entry>
         \(-7(7)\)
        </entry>
      </row>
    </tbody></tgroup></table>
  
  <para>You have studied this process earlier when you learned the difference of squares.</para>
  <para>In this lesson, we will reverse this process to rewrite the <term data-schema-version="“1.0”" class="glossary-tooltip">standard form</term> of a <term data-schema-version="“1.0”" class="glossary-tooltip">quadratic expression</term> into its <term data-schema-version="“1.0”" class="glossary-tooltip">factored form</term>.</para>
  <para>It is important to remember the <term data-schema-version="“1.0”" class="glossary-tooltip">linear term</term>, or middle term of a quadratic trinomial, equals zero only holds true for the <term data-schema-version="“1.0”" class="glossary-tooltip">difference of squares</term>. It does not work for an expression such as \(x^2+25\). (In fact, there is no way to multiply two binomials that would result in this expression.)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Example 3</emphasis></para>
  <para>Is \((x + 5)^2\) equivalent to \(x^2+25\)?</para>
  <para>Let's find the equivalent expression for \((x + 5)^2\) or \((x + 5)(x + 5)\).</para>
  <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}\left(x+5\right)^2&amp;=&amp;\left(x+5\right)\left(x+5\right)\\&amp;&amp;\\&amp;=&amp;x^2+5x+5x+25\\&amp;&amp;\\&amp;=&amp;x^2+10x+25\\&amp;&amp;\end{array}\)
    Since \(x^2+ 10x + 25\) is not equivalent to \(x^2+25\), these expressions are not the same.</para>
  <note class="try look-at-me"/>
  <title>Try It: Recognizing the Expanded Product of the Difference of Two Squares</title>
  <example><exercise>
    <problem>
      
      <para>Is the expression \((8 − 7)(8 + 7)\) equivalent to the expression \(8^2−7^2\)? Show your reasoning using the distributive property, or FOIL.</para>
      <para>Write down your answers, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
    
    </problem><solution>
    <para>Here is how to recognize the expanded product of the difference of two squares:</para>
    
    <para>Yes. Here is how to use the difference of squares to show equivalency between the expressions:</para>
    
    <para>\((8 − 7)(8 + 7) = 8^2+ 56 − 56 − 7^2= 8^2 − 7^2\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
  </example><example><exercise>
    <problem>
  
      <para>Is \((x + 4)(x + 4)\) equivalent to \(x^2+ 4^2\)? Support your answer with a diagram.</para>
      <para>Write down your answers, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
    
    </problem><solution>
    <para>Here is how to recognize the expanded product of the difference of two squares:</para>
    
    <para>No. The expressions are not equivalent.</para>
    
    <table class="os-raise-doubleheadertable">
     
      <tgroup cols="3"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis/></entry><entry><emphasis>
            \(x\)
         </emphasis> </entry><entry><emphasis>
           4
         </emphasis> </entry></row><row>
          <entry><emphasis>
            \(x\)
         </emphasis> </entry><entry>
            \(x^2\)
          </entry>
          <entry>
           \(4x\)
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry><emphasis>
          4
         </emphasis> </entry><entry>
          \(4x\)
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para>\(4(4)\)</para>
          </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    
    
    
    <para>\((x + 4)(x + 4) = x^2 + 8x + 4^2 = x^2 + 8x + 16\)</para>
    
    <para>It is not equivalent to \(x^2 + 4^2 = x^2 + 16\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
  </example></section>
</content>
